# =============================================================================
# Medora API — DirectAdmin cron entries
# =============================================================================
#
# DirectAdmin shared hosting has no Supervisor and no long-running daemon, so the panel owns
# exactly two cron entries and routes/console.php decides what actually runs inside them:
#
#   1. `schedule:run`        — every minute; runs whatever is due (cart purge, token pruning,
#                              failed-job pruning, the nightly database dump).
#   2. `queue:work --stop-when-empty`
#                            — every minute; drains whatever is waiting and exits. `--stop-when-empty`
#                              is what keeps this a cron job instead of a daemon, and `--max-time`
#                              keeps one run from overlapping the next.
#
# Replace both placeholders below before pasting:
#
#   /usr/local/php83/bin/php        the PHP binary DirectAdmin selected for the domain
#                                   (PHP Selector shows the path; it changes per PHP version)
#   /home/USER/domains/api.example.com
#                                   the absolute path where the application lives
#                                   (the *application* folder, i.e. the one holding `artisan`,
#                                   NOT its public/ subfolder)
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Raw crontab block (paste into DirectAdmin → Advanced Features → Cron Jobs → "Edit crontab")
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# MAILTO=""
# * * * * * /usr/local/php83/bin/php /home/USER/domains/api.example.com/artisan schedule:run --no-interaction >> /dev/null 2>&1
# * * * * * /usr/local/php83/bin/php /home/USER/domains/api.example.com/artisan queue:work --stop-when-empty --tries=3 --max-time=55 >> /dev/null 2>&1
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The same two jobs as DirectAdmin UI fields
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Job 1 — scheduler
#   Minute:  *        Hour: *        Day of Month: *        Month: *        Day of Week: *
#   Command: /usr/local/php83/bin/php /home/USER/domains/api.example.com/artisan schedule:run --no-interaction
#
# Job 2 — queue worker
#   Minute:  *        Hour: *        Day of Month: *        Month: *        Day of Week: *
#   Command: /usr/local/php83/bin/php /home/USER/domains/api.example.com/artisan queue:work --stop-when-empty --tries=3 --max-time=55
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Notes
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# * The scheduler writes to storage/logs/laravel.log. If the panel's cron output is mailed to you,
#   keep the `>> /dev/null 2>&1` redirect so a healthy minute is silent; remove it temporarily
#   while diagnosing.
#
# * Every scheduled task uses withoutOverlapping(), which takes a cache lock. With CACHE_STORE=database
#   that lock lives in the `cache` table, so a slow `db:backup` cannot stack up behind itself.
#
# * A queued job that keeps failing is retried 3 times and recorded in `failed_jobs`.
#   `php artisan queue:failed` lists it; `queue:prune-failed` (scheduled daily) clears old ones.
#
# * Mail: with MAIL_MAILER=smtp the queue drains in these cron minutes, so a signup email can
#   take up to a minute. That is normal for shared hosting and is why the queue is not `sync`.
#
# * Check the entries are firing:
#       php artisan schedule:list          # what the scheduler would run
#       php artisan queue:monitor default  # pending jobs
#   and confirm a row appears in `cache` under a `framework/schedule-*` key.
